This project is checked with `whitehat-squad` (`whsquad`), a security scanner built for
AI-generated code: leaked keys, open Supabase/Firebase tables, missing auth, unsafe LLM usage,
risky dependencies.

- Before treating a change as done, run `npx whitehat-squad scan . --format json --out .whsquad/report.json`.
  Exit code `1` means findings at or above `high` severity.
- Then run `npx whitehat-squad fix .` and read the plan. Work through its steps IN ORDER, one commit
  per step (rotate leaked secrets first, then data exposure, auth, injection, supply chain, hardening).
- For low/medium-confidence findings run `npx whitehat-squad fix . --format triage` and answer each
  one adversarially (CONFIRMED / REJECTED with a reason / NEEDS-HUMAN) before acting. Never modify
  code during triage. If a step needs a decision only the user can make, stop and ask.
- For live feedback while coding, suggest the user run `npx whitehat-squad watch .` in a side
  terminal; it re-scans on save and prints only what changed.
- After touching auth, a database, secrets, file uploads, or an LLM call, run the scan immediately,
  not only at the end of the session.
- Rotate, don't just delete, any leaked secret the scan finds. Fix RLS/auth gaps server-side, never
  by disabling the protection. Never silence a finding with `whsquad-ignore` unless the user
  explicitly confirms it's a deliberate exception.
- Verify each step with `npx whitehat-squad verify <RULE-ID> .` and report the result as given — don't
  claim the project is "secure" beyond what was actually verified.
- Live scans (`whsquad scan https://...`) need ownership proof first and are read-only. Only point
  them at the user's own site, never a third party's.
